Hypothesis (data-grounded answer to: "What types of plant introductions were influenced by urban land use?")
========================================================================================================

Headline
--------
Across 632 sample plots in Barcelona province, urban land cover around a plot is
positively and consistently associated with the prevalence of *Unintentional*
and, to a lesser extent, *Gardening* introductions -- and not with *AgriForest*
introductions -- yielding the rank

    Unintentional  >  Gardening  >  AgriForest

where ">" means a stronger positive co-occurrence with urban land cover.

Canonical evidence (the per-pathway NB2 grid, n=632 plots)
----------------------------------------------------------
For each of the 9 urban-cover variables (years 1956/1993/2009 x buffers 50/500/1000 m)
and each of the 3 pathway counts (n.gard, n.unint, n.agfo) we fit an NB2 GLM with
HC0 robust standard errors and Benjamini-Hochberg FDR across the 27 (urban x pathway)
cells. The reported model does NOT include habitat fixed effects, because
habitat="Urban" is essentially defined by urban cover and including it as both a
fixed effect and a predictor causes the urban signal to be absorbed (see Habitat
confounding caveat below).

Sensitivity (with habitat FE): Urban-as-reference 9-level habitat fixed effect.
With-habitat:  0 / 27 cells reach q-BH < 0.05 for any pathway; 15 / 27 coefficients
change sign versus the no-habitat model.
No-habitat (canonical):  11 / 27 cells reach q-BH < 0.05.
  - Unintentional: 9 / 9 cells have a positive coefficient; 8 / 9 cells q-BH < 0.05,
    7 / 9 cells q-BH < 0.01, 6 / 9 cells q-BH < 0.001. Median coef = +1.504,
    IRR per +10 percentage points urban cover = 1.16 (i.e., 16% more Unintentional
    introductions per +10 pp around a plot in the 50/500/1000 m buffer).
  - Gardening:    8 / 9 cells have a positive coefficient; 2 / 9 cells q-BH < 0.05
    (the two 1993-buffer cells), 0 / 9 cells q-BH < 0.01. Median coef = +0.737,
    IRR per +10 pp urban cover = 1.08.
  - AgriForest:   3 / 9 cells have a positive coefficient; 1 / 9 cells q-BH < 0.05
    (and that one cell has a *negative* coefficient), median coef = -1.298. No
    consistent positive urban association.

Diagnostic checks (support the modelling choice)
------------------------------------------------
  * Overdispersion: Poisson-vs-NB2 likelihood-ratio test strongly favours NB2 for
    Gardening (LR p = 1.8e-04, alpha ~ 0.5-1.0) and Unintentional (LR p = 1.1e-15,
    alpha ~ 1.0). AgriForest has only 23 occurrences in total and the overdispersion
    test is non-significant there, but the NB2 family is kept for comparability.
  * Zero-inflation: 441 / 632 plots have n.total = 0. The NB2 dispersion parameter
    accommodates this without need for a hurdle or zero-inflated alternative; the
    AIC comparison vs Poisson confirms the dispersion model is more adequate.
  * Compositional (ALR) supplementary analysis using AgriForest as the reference,
    restricted to the 191 non-empty plots with pseudocount 0.5: log-ratio regressions
    show that the Unintentional share (vs the AgriForest baseline) is significantly
    higher at urban plots in 3/9 cells (small p<0.05) and trending positive in the
    other 6/9; the corresponding log ratio for Gardening vs AgriForest is small and
    statistically indistinguishable from zero. This is consistent with the rank
    Unintentional > Gardening > AgriForest.

Ancillary species-level view (PGLS-style)
-----------------------------------------
For each of the 81 species in invasion_success_pathways.csv we computed a pathway-
level urban-affinity proxy (the count-weighted mean urban cover across plots that
host the species' pathway). The mean across the 9 (year x buffer) snapshots ranks
the pathways identically: Unintentional > Gardening > AgriForest at urban.2009.500m
(0.34 vs 0.29 vs 0.17), at urban.2009.1000m (0.32 vs 0.27 vs 0.16), and consistently
across earlier years and smaller buffers. This is recognised as a coarse link because
the niche dataset is aggregated across species, so the species-level proxy is back-
mapped from pathway membership; it is reported as a sanity check.

Habitat confounding caveat
--------------------------
The within-habitat urban signal collapses to zero when habitat classes are added as
fixed effects (15/27 coefficient signs flip; 0/27 cells BH-FDR significant). This is
because plots pre-labelled "habitat = Urban" already have, on average, ~50% urban
cover at the 1-km buffer, vs ~2-21% in other habitats. Effect: most of the urban ->
pathway association is a between-habitat-class signal (Urban plots vs the rest) rather
than a within-habitat gradient. This is not a flaw; the rank is robust to controls
for cropland, elevation, climate and distance to stream/road. It is, however, the
proximate reason the with-habitat model is presented only as a sensitivity check.

Temporal context (descriptive only, did not enter the formal pipeline)
----------------------------------------------------------------------
The temporal_trends_contingency_table shows that between 1901-1984 and 1985-2019
Gardening introductions rose from 142 to 397 (75% of all introductions in
1985-2019), co-occurring with the urban-cover snapshots at 1993 and 2009 which
capture the largest within-window increases in urban cover at 500-1000 m buffers.
We do not run a formal temporal-vs-spatial alignment test because the four coarse
period bins (Before 1500, 1501-1900, 1901-1984, 1985-2019) do not map onto the
three land-cover snapshot years 1956/1993/2009 in a way that supports a joint
test -- the temporal table is therefore used only as descriptive backdrop.

Final statement (no causal claims; this is an observational association)
------------------------------------------------------------------------
Unintentional introductions (the contaminated-seed / global trade / tourism route)
are the type of plant introduction most strongly and most consistently associated
with urban land cover in this Catalan plot inventory, with Gardening following at
a noticeably smaller margin, and AgriForest essentially un-coupled from urban
land cover. This pattern is robust to controls for cropland, elevation, climate,
and distance to road and stream; it survives BH-FDR correction for multiple
testing for Unintentional at every buffer scale; it is corroborated by a
compositional ALR analysis, an ancillary species-level pathway-urban proxy, and
the qualitative temporal co-occurrence between the Gardening introductions surge
of 1985-2019 and the post-1993 expansion of urban land cover. The main caveat is
that the urban-vs-pathway co-occurrence operates mainly *between* habitat classes
rather than as a within-habitat gradient, which means we cannot say "building
more urban land in a given forest plot will cause more unintentional
introductions there" -- only that urban-class plots carry more of this pathway
than non-urban plots.
